---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// AboutPage JSON-LD references the canonical Organization node defined on
// index.astro and iispeed.com via @id. Cross-property identity lets Google
// reconcile the same legal entity wherever it appears.
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: 'About — mod_pagespeed 2.1',
  url: 'https://modpagespeed.com/about/',
  description:
    'The team behind mod_pagespeed 2.1, introduced as ModPageSpeed 2.0: We-Amp B.V., a Dutch software company founded in 2012 that helped build ngx_pagespeed and maintained mod_pagespeed, and today maintains the PageSpeed optimization lineage — led by Otto van der Schaaf, an Apache PageSpeed committer and IPMC member.',
  mainEntity: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    '@id': 'https://we-amp.com/#organization',
    name: 'We-Amp B.V.',
    url: 'https://we-amp.com',
    logo: 'https://we-amp.com/images/weamp.svg',
    description:
      'Dutch software company building server-side web optimization technology. Maintainer of mod_pagespeed 2.1.',
    foundingDate: '2012',
    founder: {
      '@type': 'Person',
      name: 'Otto van der Schaaf',
      sameAs: ['https://www.linkedin.com/in/oschaaf', 'https://github.com/oschaaf'],
    },
    address: {
      '@type': 'PostalAddress',
      addressLocality: 'Castricum',
      addressCountry: 'NL',
    },
    identifier: [{ '@type': 'PropertyValue', propertyID: 'KvK', value: '57898138' }],
    sameAs: [
      'https://we-amp.com',
      'https://iispeed.com',
      'https://github.com/We-Amp',
      'https://github.com/oschaaf',
    ],
    brand: {
      '@type': 'Brand',
      name: 'mod_pagespeed 2.1',
      alternateName: 'ModPageSpeed 2.0',
    },
  },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — mod_pagespeed 2.1"
  description="Built by We-Amp B.V. — a Dutch web-optimization company that helped build ngx_pagespeed and maintains mod_pagespeed 2.1, ngx_pagespeed, and IISpeed."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  {/* Hero */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-20">
    <h1 class="h1-hero text-text-body">The team behind mod_pagespeed.</h1>
    <p class="lede mt-8 max-w-2xl">
      mod_pagespeed 2.1 is built by We-Amp B.V., a Dutch software company founded in 2012 in
      Castricum, on the coast north of Amsterdam. It's led by Otto van der Schaaf, an Apache
      PageSpeed committer and IPMC member who helped build ngx_pagespeed, maintained mod_pagespeed,
      and shipped open-source PageSpeed releases. One product in two parts, one shared optimization
      core, and a line that traces back to the original mod_pagespeed in 2010.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* Lineage */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 border-t border-border">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">The lineage</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-text-muted">
      <p>
        mod_pagespeed was an open-source server-side optimization framework originally built and
        released by Google in 2010. The original team &mdash; Joshua Marantz, Jeff Kaufman, Maks
        Orlovich, Bryan McQuade and others &mdash; shipped an Apache module that did things the rest
        of the web hadn't figured out yet: server-side image transcoding, critical-CSS inlining,
        cache-extension, JS minification, dozens of transforms across a content pipeline that
        thousands of operators put in front of production traffic.
      </p>
      <p>
        It set the standard. It's no longer actively developed upstream &mdash; Google retired the
        project in 2020. The <a
          href="/psol/"
          class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
          >optimization libraries from the original mod_pagespeed</a
        > are the shared core of both parts. The <a
          href="/"
          class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline"
          >mod_pagespeed 2.1 module</a
        > carries the proven line forward in-process on Apache, nginx and IIS; the 2.0 re-architecture
        wrapped the same rewriters in a worker and cache architecture, and that worker is the other part.
        The rewriters that decide how to compress an image or rewrite a stylesheet are the ones the original
        team built and proved.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* The company */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 border-t border-border">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">The company</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-text-muted">
      <p>
        We-Amp B.V. is a Dutch private limited company, registered at the KvK under 57898138,
        founded in Castricum in 2012. The specialty has always been web performance: <a
          href="/self-hosted-image-optimization/"
          class="text-interactive hover:text-interactive-hover underline">image optimization</a
        >, content rewriting, cache architecture, the boring infrastructure that decides whether a
        page is fast or slow.
      </p>
      <p>
        Today We-Amp is led by Otto van der Schaaf, an Apache PageSpeed committer and IPMC member
        who helped build ngx_pagespeed and drove the project's Apache incubation. After the
        incubator was archived and upstream went quiet, We-Amp carried the lineage forward &mdash;
        shipping optimization code continuously since 2010, across Apache, nginx, Envoy and IIS
        ports of the 1.x line. The 2.0 re-architecture was We-Amp's independent rebuild, and
        mod_pagespeed 2.1 brings it together with the module as one product: one shared optimization
        core, one Cyclone Cache library underneath both parts.
      </p>
      <p>
        Self-hosted software fits the way we work: you run the binary on your infrastructure, you
        control the upgrade timing, we don't sit in your request path.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Where AI fits */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 border-t border-border">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Where AI fits, and where it doesn't</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-text-muted">
      <p>
        Inside the binary: small LightGBM models &mdash; one per format, JPEG, WebP, AVIF &mdash;
        predict the encoder quality that hits a target SSIMULACRA2 perceptual score. Compiled to C
        through TL2cgen, around five microseconds per call. No model downloads, no runtime
        dependency, no outbound call in the request path. Every variant is verified against the
        original with SSIMULACRA2 before it reaches your cache. Don't want it?
        <code>--no-learned-quality-jpeg</code>, <code>--no-learned-quality-webp</code>,
        <code>--no-learned-quality-avif</code>. Per format.
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside the binary: agents watch security advisories, performance research and upstream
        changes, and brief the team. A human approves before anything user-visible ships. No release
        goes out without that sign-off.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Why this matters */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 border-t border-border">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Why self-hosted, in 2026</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-text-muted">
      <p>
        The 2026 web is HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, container orchestration, nginx in front of nearly
        everything, AVIF in every browser worth optimizing for. The performance story is no longer
        "the network is slow" &mdash; it's "your origin is doing too much work and shipping too many
        bytes." Server-side optimization should live next to your origin, not in someone else's data
        center two hops away.
      </p>
      <p>
        mod_pagespeed 2.1 reflects that reality. The module serves and the optimizer worker
        optimizes; they talk over a local socket, and neither phones home with request data. There
        is no third-party proxy in the path. There is no CDN dependency. It makes no outbound
        request of its own accord — the only egress is what the operator configures.
      </p>
      <p>
        Operators in 2026 have absorbed the trade-offs of edge platforms and managed services, and a
        growing number want some things back on their own boxes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* Get in touch */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 border-t border-border">
    <h2 class="h2-section text-text-body">Get in touch</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 text-text-muted">
      Questions about the product, the company, or the lineage &mdash; email or use the form.
      Pricing is published; no sales call required to read it.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
      <a href="/contact/" class="btn-primary"> Contact </a>
      <a
        href="/pricing/"
        class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-lg border border-border-strong px-6 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-text-body hover:bg-bg-secondary transition-colors"
      >
        See pricing
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-10 text-xs text-text-annotation max-w-2xl">
      mod_pagespeed is an open-source project originally developed at Google. mod_pagespeed 2.1 is
      developed by We-Amp B.V. and is not affiliated with or endorsed by Google.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
